就在冷开场，在剧集刚开始的时候，你就会想：这到底是什么？

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:05.740
There's something we are going on here.
这里面肯定有些什么情况。

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.899
What do we, we're in Ted's shoes, we don't know what's wrong either.
我们代入泰德的视角，我们也不知道哪里不对劲。

00:08:08.899 --> 00:08:20.180
And the audience is very much, I mean, I always thought I was a portal, my character was a portal for the audience, but very much in this one, because the audience is just as confused as him, because you withhold, I mean, how many of your mother was like?
而且观众非常有……我的意思是，我一直以为我是个通道，我的角色是观众的通道，但在这一集里尤其如此，因为观众和他一样困惑，因为你们有所保留，我是说，《老爸老妈浪漫史》有多少次是这样的？

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:25.579
It's legitimately confusing, and so good at withholding information, you know?
这真的让人困惑，而且你们在隐瞒信息方面太有一套了，你知道吗？

00:08:25.579 --> 00:08:27.300
It's on a need-to-know basis.
这是按需知悉的。

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:31.259
It is, the whole beginning of the episode is maddening.
确实，这一集的整个开头都让人抓狂。

00:08:31.259 --> 00:08:33.860
And I kind of forgot what it was gonna be.
我有点忘了后来会发生什么。

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.500
I hadn't watched this one in years.
这一集我好几年没看了。

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.179
And I was like, what is her thing again?
然后我就想：她到底有什么毛病来着？

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:38.500
And then I was like on the edge of my seat.
接着我看得心潮澎湃。

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:39.340
I was like, right, right.
我心想：对，没错。

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.179
She talks me too much.
她跟我说得太多了。

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:45.980
And we've abridged that in the cold open, but later we see the fullness of what she was really saying.
我们在开场白里删节过，但后来我们看到了她真正想表达的完整内容。

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:53.059
Have you ever had the experience of a dear friend dating someone that drove you bonkers?
你有没有过这种经历：好朋友在跟一个让你抓狂的人谈恋爱？

00:08:53.059 --> 00:08:55.700
Or did you tell them or not tell them?
或者你有没有告诉过他们，还是没说？

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:59.860
And what is your feeling about the ethics of telling someone that.
你对把这种事告诉别人的道德看法是什么？

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:02.659
I mean, Ted is devastated when he finds out, right?
我是说，泰德发现的时候整个人都崩溃了，对吧？

00:09:02.659 --> 00:09:06.019
Was he, was he, but I guess you could argue it either way.
他是吗，真的是吗？不过我想你可以从各个角度来争论。

00:09:06.019 --> 00:09:10.460
Like are his friends doing him a service and saying, you'll see this eventually.
比如他的朋友是不是在帮他，说你以后就会明白了。

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.059
So we're just going to fast forward here.
所以我们这里直接快进。

00:09:12.059 --> 00:09:13.059
You're in the infatuation.
你正处于迷恋之中。

00:09:13.059 --> 00:09:14.940
I like that idea of the goggles.
我挺喜欢那种“滤镜”的说法。

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:16.740
What does Marne say or the shoot?
马恩说了什么来着？或者说……

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:17.580
I'm forgetting.
我给忘。

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:18.779
You want to have sex goggles or whatever.
你想说的是“荷尔蒙滤镜”什么的。

00:09:18.779 --> 00:09:24.220
Whatever it was like the idea of like, we're just going to take these things off here and just kind of tell you what's up.
不管怎样，就像是：我们要把这些东西拿掉，然后直接告诉你真相。

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:29.500
So I guess they think they're doing Ted a service, but I don't know, what do you think the ethics are?
所以我想他们觉得自己是在帮泰德的忙，但我不知道，你觉得这里的道德界限在哪里？

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:43.100
I mean, people should be allowed to discover it for themselves, and maybe they never will discover it, and then that's who you should marry, I guess, because you love them so much, and so for so in such an enduring way that you never see their flaw, or you embrace their flaw.
我是说，应该允许人们自己去发现，也许他们永远不会发现，那我想这就是你该结婚的人，因为你如此深爱着对方，以至于这种爱历久弥新，让你永远看不到对方的缺点，或者你能够包容他们的缺点。

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:47.299
I don't know, I found myself wondering about this philosophical question too.
我不知道，我也曾纳闷过这个哲学问题。

00:09:47.299 --> 00:09:56.179
You know, it's funny, someone, I don't want to get into specifics, but someone we both know, I broke up with a girlfriend.
你知道，这很有趣，有个——我不想扯到具体细节，但我们都认识的一个人，我跟一个女朋友分手了。

00:09:56.179 --> 00:09:59.279
This is in like the later seasons and she was not on the show.
这是在比较后面的几季，而且她没有在节目里出现。

00:09:59.279 --> 00:10:05.580
She was like, no one, you know, but this person, this mutual friend of ours said, oh, good.
她就像是……没人认识，但这个人，我们的一个共同朋友说，哦，太好了。

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:07.580
You don't want to be with her, right?
你不想跟她在一起，对吧？

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:12.679
And she went home and her husband said, you can never say that to someone.
然后她回家后，她丈夫说，你绝不能对别人说这种话。

00:10:12.679 --> 00:10:13.879
And she said, why is it?
然后她说，为什么？

00:10:13.879 --> 00:10:15.179
What if they get back together?
万一他们复合了呢？

00:10:15.179 --> 00:10:15.980
Yeah.
对啊。

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:17.779
And we got back together.
然后我们复合了。

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:19.980
Oh my God.
我的天哪。

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.080
We got back together for about seven or eight months.
我们复合了大概七八个月。

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.379
And I think she was pretty horrified.
我想她当时肯定吓坏了。

00:10:25.379 --> 00:10:41.715
But wait, wait, wait, wait did in that moment where she said that your friend said that wasn't where the flaws pointed out Was it like good you can't be with her cuz barra or was the canner worms wasn't open?
等等，等等，在你朋友说那话的那个时刻，是不是没有指出具体的缺点？是像“太好了，你不能跟她在一起，因为巴拉巴拉”这样的，还是说潘多拉的魔盒根本没打开？

00:10:41.715 --> 00:10:46.977
No She just thought it was she just more thought it wasn't a match.
不，她只是觉得……她更倾向于认为俩人不合适。

00:10:46.977 --> 00:10:51.375
She didn't like okay It wasn't like quite spoiler alert.
她不喜欢……好吧，不算是什么剧透警告。

00:10:51.375 --> 00:10:53.339
It wasn't this mechanism.
机制不是这样的，不是那种情况，但就感觉有点棘手。

00:10:53.339 --> 00:11:00.250
It wasn't not that but it was like a little tough You're walking a fine line here, Fred.
弗雷德，你这是在走钢丝啊。

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:04.569
Yeah You're gonna tell me a dinner with the full story.
行，你得在吃晚饭时把完整的故事告诉我。

00:11:04.569 --> 00:11:11.010
I can't wait for dinner now Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
我现在等不及要吃晚饭了。嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，播客《Hyper-Fixed》的主持人。

00:11:11.009 --> 00:11:14.710
Each week we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周我们都会收集听众的难题并试着帮他们解决。

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:17.669
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这些问题：我30岁了，但不敢在纽约开车。

00:11:17.669 --> 00:11:21.389
Or why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者为什么我挂倒挡时不能调节汽车音响的音量？

00:11:21.389 --> 00:11:23.950
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:29.940
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有什么问题，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的背后隐藏系统。

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.419
That's the hyper-fixed podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的播客《Hyper-Fixed》。

00:11:32.419 --> 00:11:47.649
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at hyperfixedpod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台或hyperfixedpod.com上找到它。

00:11:47.649 --> 00:11:51.309
And now, back to the show.
现在，让我们回到节目中。

00:11:51.309 --> 00:11:54.230
I think it's one thing when you're casually dating someone.
我觉得当你只是随便约会时是一回事。

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:57.070
It's another thing if there's like a ring has been presented.
如果已经求婚了，那就是另一码事了。

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:57.909
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:11:57.909 --> 00:12:02.350
Hey, right, this is a very different question depending on where in the timeline we are.
嘿，对啊，这取决于我们处于时间线的哪个阶段，这是一个完全不同的问题。

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:04.190
Because Ted has just met this woman.
因为泰德才刚刚认识这个女人。

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:06.389
And I guess you can sort of say it.
我想你大概可以这么说。

00:12:06.389 --> 00:12:10.190
And to be fair, they were not telling him until he prided out of them.
而且平心而论，他们一开始没告诉他，直到他逼问出来。

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:14.629
And it's very funny, his buyer's remorse once he knows, like, why did you tell me?
而且很有趣，他知道了之后的这种买家后悔症，就像是，你为什么要告诉我？

00:12:14.629 --> 00:12:17.190
You know I didn't really want to know which to Marshall.
你知道我其实并不想知道马歇尔的这个缺点。

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:25.950
But also, I think if Barney or Robin or Lily, one of them thought this, he might have been able to brush it off.
但同时，我觉得如果是巴尼、罗宾或莉莉，他们其中一人这么想，他可能还能不放在心上。

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:29.909
The uniformity of the opinion around this thing is so strong.
大家对这件事的看法竟然如此一致。

00:12:29.909 --> 00:12:34.110
It's like his four best friends in the world are like, she won't stop talking, this is unbearable.
就好像他世界上最好的四个朋友都在说，她喋喋不休，这简直让人无法忍受。

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:34.610
Yeah.
是啊。

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:40.583
It must have been where do you guys come up with the, is it like the, the glass breaking or the mirror breaking?
当时你们是怎么想出那个概念的，是类似玻璃碎裂还是镜子碎裂？

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:40.850
Yeah.
对。

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:43.995
Just, just, just the idea of like you can't put it back together again.
就是，就是，那种一旦破了就无法复原的想法。

00:12:43.995 --> 00:12:51.439
Just that idea like it's something has, and illusion, as an illusion has shattered and you can't put it by, we're, we're looking for the perfect kind of device of that.
就是那种感觉，有什么东西，一种幻想，像幻想一样破灭了，然后你无法复原，我们一直在寻找完美的视觉表现方式。

00:12:51.439 --> 00:12:55.605
And I really like that, that sound effect and that's sort of the feeling of that's the moment.
我真的很喜欢那个音效，那正是那种感觉，就是那个瞬间。

00:12:55.605 --> 00:12:56.668
There's no turning back.
没有回头路了。

00:12:56.389 --> 00:12:57.389
Yeah.
对。

00:12:57.389 --> 00:12:59.350
You can't put that toothpaste back in the tube.
覆水难收。

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:00.809
Barney's metaphor was a car, right?
巴尼的比喻是一辆车，对吧？

00:13:00.809 --> 00:13:03.269
It was you're driving, and there's a blind spot in the car.
意思是你在开车，车上有个盲区。

00:13:03.269 --> 00:13:08.750
There's just a blind spot towards this person, and it feels like a real thing.
对这个人就是有一种盲区，而且感觉很真实。

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:09.830
I definitely feel like it's real.
我绝对觉得这很真实。

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:16.070
I think another good hemiumism besides many mysteries is legitimately interesting, juicy debates.
我觉得另一个好用的委婉说法——除了各种谜团外——是真正有趣、有料的辩论。

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:17.629
This is a philosophical debate, right?
这是一个哲学辩论，对吧？

00:13:17.629 --> 00:13:18.470
Totally.
完全是。

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:28.629
Do you say the thing, when do you say the thing, when does it pass the point, when you can say the thing, what if you say the thing they break up and they go back together, all of this is an ethic, this is a study in ethics.
你什么时候该说出来，什么时候该说，什么时候错过了说出来的时机，如果你说出来之后他们分手了又复合了会怎样，所有这些都是伦理学，这是一项伦理学研究。

00:13:28.629 --> 00:13:34.750
It really, I don't know whether the university class on how I met your mother could do a juicy seminar in this one.
真的，我不知道关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》的大学课程能不能在这集里开个精彩的研讨会。

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:35.590
Yeah.
对。

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:39.389
And I love how everyone just turns on each other from just everyone's flaws.
而且我喜欢大家如何仅仅因为彼此的缺点而反目成仇。

00:13:39.389 --> 00:13:48.429
And it becomes this study in acceptance versus honesty or saying something versus not saying something.
这演变成了一场关于接纳与诚实，或者说直言不讳与缄口不言的研究。

00:13:48.429 --> 00:13:51.870
It starts to, in fact, it's almost like this contagion.
它开始，事实上，这几乎就像是一种传染病。

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:53.549
It's almost like an outbreak story.
这几乎就像是一个疫情爆发的故事。

00:13:53.549 --> 00:14:02.470
That's like slowly the idea of this glass breaking disease starts like infiltrating the group and just the way everyone turns on each other is really funny.
就像“玻璃碎裂”这种病的想法开始慢慢渗透进这个群体，大家互相针对的方式真的太搞笑了。

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:03.309
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:14:03.309 --> 00:14:04.149
It's a good gang episode.
这是一集很棒的群戏。

00:14:04.149 --> 00:14:07.870
Like the whole gang is- It is a good gang episode, everyone is in the same story.
就像整个死党——这确实是一集很棒的群戏，每个人都在同一个故事里。

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:08.370
Yeah.
对。

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:09.178
Was that fun?
那很有趣吗？

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:09.830
Do you like an episode?
你喜欢拍这样的一集吗？

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:14.029
Do you like acting in an episode like that where it's, well, it's great that it started with Ted.
比如从泰德开始这很棒。

00:14:14.029 --> 00:14:15.590
It's great that Ted was the mystery.
泰德成为谜团这很棒。

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:18.870
But then when everyone starts turning on each other, it's there was a good energy.
但后来当大家开始互相针对时，有一种很好的能量。

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:20.350
It was very entertaining to watch.
看的时候非常有趣。

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:25.470
And the flashbacks kind of filling in the lore, as I say, like those were super fun.
而且那些填充背景设定的闪回，正如我所说，那些超级有趣。

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:26.389
Those were fun.
那些很有趣。

00:14:26.389 --> 00:14:34.590
Did you, so I'm curious like writer's room, like that's a great writer's room prompt, like what's annoying about each of these people?
你当时——所以我很好奇编剧室是怎么运作的，比如这是一个很棒的编剧室灵感，每个人身上都有什么烦人的地方？

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:38.629
And I think you had established Marshall the singer, right?
我想你们当时已经塑造了爱唱歌的马歇尔，对吧？

00:14:38.629 --> 00:14:42.750
Yes, that felt very natural because we were really just calling out something that was already there.
是的，那感觉非常自然，因为我们只是在指出原本就存在的东西。

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:43.590
But you know what?
但你知道吗？

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:55.389
His singing was at least in season one was established like he was just by himself, kind of amusing himself, but it was funny to open it up, like he's actually doing it around them the whole time.
他的歌声至少在第一季里就已经确立了，他就像是独自一人，自娱自乐，但把它公开会很有趣，就好像他其实一直都在他们面前这么做。

00:14:55.389 --> 00:15:07.070
I think Ted, like by season three, you guys were very much into Ted, the intellect, Ted, the kind of snooty, you know, as neat or whatever.
我觉得泰德，到了第三季左右，你们非常喜欢塑造泰德知识分子的一面，那种有点自命不凡的泰德，你知道，文青什么的。

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:09.950
So I think you were leaning into that.
所以我认为你们在往那个方向倾斜。

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:10.870
You know what's so funny?
你知道最搞笑的是什么吗？

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:21.409
I was, I always felt a little bit, it's so weird cause I'm watching this shit, here, listen, this is a shout out to my mother-in-law.
我以前，我总是觉得有点——这太奇怪了，因为我在看这个狗屎，听着，这是向我岳母致敬。

00:15:21.409 --> 00:15:23.090
My mother-in-law doesn't like swearing.
我岳母不喜欢说脏话。

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:24.590
and whenever I say.
每当我说话的时候。

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:25.870
You hear her voice in your head?
你脑海里会浮现她的声音？

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:26.710
Because you say.
因为你说。

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:29.230
I hear her, I hear her, she listens to this show.
我能听到她，我能听到她，她会收听这个节目。

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:30.070
Yeah.
对。

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:32.230
And I hear her thinking, I wish you wouldn't.
我能听到她在想，我希望你别这样。

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:33.070
She doesn't even sound like that.
她听起来才不那样呢。

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:33.909
I wish you wouldn't.
我希望你别这样。

00:15:33.909 --> 00:15:34.750
Let's hate me that.
别那样讨厌我。

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:35.590
That's not what her voice sounds like.
她的声音才不是那样的。

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:36.750
Sorry, Vivian.
抱歉，薇薇安。

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:51.070
Whenever I think about, whenever I would think about, or whenever I, you handed us the stuff, the TED as kind of intellectual snob, I knew it worked because it got huge laughs from, you know, the table reads and the crew.
每当我想起，每当我想到，或者每当你们把剧本交给我们，把泰德塑造成那种知识分子势利小人时，我知道这行得通，因为读剧本时和剧组人员都发出了巨大的笑声。

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:52.870
And I knew I was doing it well.
